{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "bs-02-time-distance-ratios-current",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Time and Distance Ratios in a Current",
    "description": "Use inverse time-speed reasoning and same-time distance ratios to solve river questions quickly.",
    "teachingApproach": "Begin with the same route taken in different times so the inverse speed-time relationship is visible. Convert between effective-speed ratios and still-water/current ratios, then add known current values and same-time distance conditions. Finish with the chapter’s long-time-ratio pattern.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 19, Boats and Streams. Approved scope: Teach that for the same distance, rowing time is inversely proportional to effective speed, while for the same time, distance is proportional to effective speed. Convert downstream:upstream speed ratios into still-water:current ratios using half-sum and half-difference. Cover ratios of still-water speed to current, upstream/downstream time ratios, fixed-current problems where the two travel times determine the boat speed, equal-time unequal-distance questions, and source patterns such as a boat taking 8 hours upstream and 6 hours downstream or taking a multiple of the time to return."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn the ratio shortcuts hidden inside boat journeys.\n\nWhen the distance stays the same, a faster river speed means less time. That single idea removes a lot of algebra.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A boat covers the same distance downstream in 3 hours and upstream in 6 hours.\n\nThe upstream trip takes twice as long, so it must be moving at half the speed.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For the same distance:\n\nSpeed ratio = reverse of time ratio.\n\nDownstream : Upstream\n= 6 : 3\n= 2 : 1.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
